{"parentUuid":"1eb070f5-20d4-4958-bed6-79d4637f74f5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CfbvSCywsAY5ojoPp8zoU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ocean tides are the rise and fall of sea levels caused primarily by the gravitational pull of the Moon and, to a lesser extent, the Sun on Earth's oceans. As the Moon orbits Earth and the Sun's gravity pulls on our planet, these celestial bodies create bulges in the ocean's surface on the side facing them and on the opposite side. This results in two high tides and two low tides occurring approximately every 24 hours and 50 minutes, which is why tidal schedules shift slightly each day. The size and timing of tides vary significantly depending on geography, with some coastal areas experiencing dramatic differences between high and low tide, while others have barely noticeable changes. Tides are crucial for marine ecosystems, affecting nutrient distribution, the exposure of tidal pools, and the behavior of countless sea creatures. They also have practical importance for shipping, fishing, and coastal communities that depend on predictable tidal patterns. Scientists have been able to predict tides with remarkable accuracy for centuries by understanding the mathematical relationships between Earth's rotation, the Moon's orbit, and gravitational forces, making tides one of the most reliable natural phenomena on our planet.\n\nEND-OF-REPLY"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1338,"cache_read_input_tokens":33746,"output_tokens":357,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":98},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1338,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":357,"cache_read_i